      "content": "Most geopolitical analysts agree that while the intervention was morally necessary to address the imminent threat of authoritarian brutality under Qaddafi, the lack of a clear exit strategy and robust post-conflict planning rendered it a strategic error rather than a pure mistake. Many argue that the U.S. should have intervened decisively but with a \"stay out of nation-building\" mandate to prevent a prolonged destabilization without a clear end goal. Ultimately, the debate hinges on whether moral imperatives justify the long-term geopolitical costs, which for the U.S. largely point to a failure of strategy rather than a failure of premise.",
